  "difficulty_rating": 1600,
  "explanation": "`return result` sits inside the `for` loop body at the same indentation as the `if`, so it executes unconditionally after just the first iteration — the function returns after looking at exactly one element, whether or not it was negative. For [3, 5, -2, 8], the first number (3) isn't negative, so `result` is still None when the loop returns on that very first pass; the -2 further along is never even inspected. For [-1, 5, 8] it happens to return the correct answer, -1, purely because the first element itself was negative. The bug is present on every call, but only visible when the first element isn't the one you're looking for.",
  "prompt": "find_first_negative is meant to scan the whole list and return the first negative number found. Step through both calls to summarize and predict each result.",
  "language": "python",
  "snippet": "def find_first_negative(nums):\n    result = None\n    for n in nums:\n        if n < 0:\n            result = n\n        return result\n\ndef summarize(nums):\n    first_neg = find_first_negative(nums)\n    return \"first negative: \" + str(first_neg)\n\nprint(summarize([3, 5, -2, 8]))\nprint(summarize([-1, 5, 8]))",
